{"request": {"input_type": "document", "model": "voyage-4", "text": "An account manager describes, in an interview about writing to existing customers, what they stress when customers raise security, how they communicate outages plainly, why they log every promise in the CRM, and what advice they would give a new account manager about reading the thread first.\n\nAccount manager: Security, more and more. I always tell Pro customers that single sign-on is included, because that is the first question their IT team asks. It unblocks the deal.\n\nInterviewer: How do you handle bad news, like an outage?\n\nAccount manager: Plain and early. Say what happened, what it affected, and when the next update comes. No marketing words, no \"we are thrilled\". And I never blame the customer's setup in writing, even when it is their setup.\n\nInterviewer: Any habits around the CRM?\n\nAccount manager: I log every promise I make in the CRM on the same day. If I told someone they get two training sessions, it goes in the notes, because the next person on the account needs to know what was promised.\n\nInterviewer: Last question. What would you tell a new account manager?\n\nAccount manager: Read the last three emails in the thread before you write. Half the mistakes I see are people answering a question that was already answered, or contradicting what a colleague promised last month."}, "vector": [-0.013078267686069012, 0.012518108822405338, 0.023986535146832466, -0.011081835255026817, 0.05071856454014778, 0.0008272986742667854, -0.023921098560094833, 0.020325424149632454, -0.009133389219641685, 0.04017720744013786, 0.039735518395900726, 0.07568834722042084, -0.0022520392667502165, 0.032565873116254807, -0.05546228215098381, 0.02905920147895813, 0.01836356148123741, -0.007383156567811966, -0.003999209031462669, 0.05113851651549339, 0.03312721475958824, 0.06511397659778595, 0.011293142102658749, -0.02097495272755623, -0.013075383380055428, -0.005681465845555067, -0.053874671459198, 0.